<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  laptop-comm.xml — EthernetKRL channel configuration for the
  pick-and-place cell. Referenced from KRL as EKI_Init("laptop-comm")
  and EKI_Open("laptop-comm"): the argument is this file's name without
  its extension.

  *** NOT VERIFIED AGAINST A CONTROLLER. ***
  No KRC exists in this project, so the EKI parser has never seen this
  file. What follows says which parts rest on KUKA's own manual and which
  are the best reading available; the second kind must be checked against
  the EthernetKRL manual's configuration chapter before anyone loads it.

  MODE: RAW BINARY, FIXED LENGTH (BinaryFixed), 16 bytes each way.

  Why binary at all. execution/protocol.py defines a 16-byte fixed-length
  frame. The file that used to be here declared XML payloads, so the
  Python side and the controller side could not have interoperated. The
  fix is the mode, not the frame: KUKA's EthernetKRL manual documents
  three connection data types, XML, binary of fixed length and a variable
  binary stream, and states that "Binary data records are not interpreted
  by the EKI and stored together in a memory", read back with
  EKI_GetString and split with CAST_FROM. A fixed-length binary record is
  therefore a native EKI capability, not a departure from EKI. The CRC-16
  trailer in bytes 14..15 is this repository's own addition, because EKI
  supplies no checksum.

  FROM THE KUKA MANUAL, and relied on here:
    * ELEMENT attribute Type="BYTE" selects a binary record of fixed
      length; Size then gives that length, in the range 1 to 3600 bytes.
      Type="STREAM" with an EOS delimiter is the variable-length variant
      and is not what this frame is.
    * Set_Flag sets $FLAG[n] when the element has been received, n in
      1..1025. It is the mechanism by which KRL learns a telegram
      arrived; the previous file had none, so the KRL side had nothing to
      trigger on. laptop_comm.src waits on $FLAG[1].
    * EXTERNAL/TYPE says whether the EXTERNAL system is server or client.
      Here the laptop is the client, so the controller is the server;
      EXTERNAL/IP and EXTERNAL/PORT are documented as ignored in that
      case, which is why they are absent.
    * Element names are upper case, attribute names are mixed case, and
      the manual notes that XML files are case sensitive. The previous
      file had this backwards.
    * ELEMENT entries are a flat list; a Tag carries a path when it needs
      one. The previous file nested ELEMENT inside ELEMENT.

  NOT CONFIRMED, and flagged rather than asserted:
    * the exact placement of the controller's own IP and PORT in server
      mode. They are given here under INTERNAL because EXTERNAL/IP and
      EXTERNAL/PORT are documented as the external system's address. The
      manual page that lists INTERNAL's children was not obtained.
    * whether BUFFERING and BUFFSIZE are attributes of INTERNAL, as
      written here, or attributes of a child element. One extracted page
      calls them attributes without giving the parent.
    * ALIVE and its Set_Flag. Included because a link-loss indication is
      worth having and omitted from the KRL loop's logic, which does not
      depend on it.

  Port 54600 and address 172.31.1.147 match configs/execution.yaml and
  ExecutionConfig's defaults. 172.31.1.x is the conventional KRC4 KLI
  subnet and the EKI server ports are conventionally 54600 to 54615.

  Byte layout of the 16-byte record, both directions, from
  execution/protocol.py. EKI does not interpret it; laptop_comm.src does.
    0      version = 0x01
    1      opcode, or status code on the reply
    2..5   x, int32, big endian, millimetres
    6..9   y, int32, big endian, millimetres
    10..11 z, int16, big endian, millimetres
    12..13 aux, uint16, big endian (vacuum per cent, or cycle time ms)
    14..15 CRC-16/MODBUS of bytes 0..13, little endian
-->
<ETHERNETKRL>

  <CONFIGURATION>
    <EXTERNAL>
      <TYPE>Client</TYPE>
    </EXTERNAL>
    <INTERNAL BUFFERING="FIFO" BUFFSIZE="16" TIMEOUT="2000">
      <IP>172.31.1.147</IP>
      <PORT>54600</PORT>
      <ALIVE Set_Flag="2"/>
    </INTERNAL>
  </CONFIGURATION>

  <RECEIVE>
    <RAW>
      <ELEMENT Tag="Buffer" Type="BYTE" Size="16" Set_Flag="1"/>
    </RAW>
  </RECEIVE>

  <SEND>
    <RAW>
      <ELEMENT Tag="Buffer" Type="BYTE" Size="16"/>
    </RAW>
  </SEND>

</ETHERNETKRL>
